      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, that's a lot better and definitely more informative. \n\nthough, looking at the length, the score is still coming in quite low for our requirements. the students really need these to be massive \"deep-dive\" modules. i'm looking for something much more exhaustive—like several pages of content for one concept so they can really sit with it.\n\ncan you take this whole explanation and expand it significantly? i want you to detail the wave-packet construction, the specific mathematics of the Fourier transform relation, the implications for the Schrodinger equation, and a very long section on the stability of the hydrogen atom. \n\ni'm really stressing this: i need the length to go up substantially to hit the library requirements, but please, i am begging you, don't just repeat yourself or use \"fluff\" to make it long. keep it high-level and rigorous, just... make it much, much more voluminous.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Expanding to such a massive depth requires delving deeply into the mathematical formalism connecting wave mechanics and uncertainty. The construction of a position-localized state (a wave packet) is central; to localize a particle precisely at time $t=0$, its wave function $\\Psi(x, 0)$ must be concentrated in a small region $\\Delta x$. Mathematically, this requires $\\Psi(x, 0)$ to be a superposition of plane waves, $\\Psi(x, 0) = \\int A(p) e^{ipx/\\hbar} dp$, where $A(p)$ is the momentum space amplitude. The spread in position $\\Delta x$ is inversely related to the spread in the momentum distribution $\\Delta p$, where the spread of $A(p)$ dictates $\\Delta p$ according to the Fourier relationship: $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\propto \\frac{\\hbar}{2}$ is a direct manifestation of the uncertainty inherent in representing a sharply localized function by its Fourier transform.\n\nThis uncertainty directly impacts the time evolution governed by the time-dependent Schrödinger Equation. If a state is perfectly localized ($\\Delta x \\to 0$), it represents an infinite range of momenta, leading to a very rapid spread of the wave",
      "source": "target",
